diagnosis_text:
A thorough four-phase sweep of the dc_clos_bgp fabric found no fault. Phase 1 reachability confirms that pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) reaches pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) right now with 0% packet loss in both directions, directly contradicting the helpdesk claim. Phase A infrastructure sweep examined all 7 devices and found every interface UP (l2_snapshot: 0 duplicate MACs, 0 downed interfaces), no ACL/nftables drop rules, no static-ARP poisoning, and both hosts properly addressed with default routes intact. Phase B BGP snapshot showed all five FRR routers — leaf_router_0_0 (AS 65200), leaf_router_0_1 (AS 65201), spine_router_0_0 (AS 65100), spine_router_0_1 (AS 65101), and super_spine_router_0 (AS 65000) — with watchfrr/zebra/bgpd running and every BGP session in Established state. The two leaf routers correctly advertise their host subnets (10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24) and no blackhole/null routes exist. TC snapshot confirmed all 16 fabric interfaces use the default fq_codel qdisc with no bandwidth throttle or corruption. Phase D pressure sweep found no stress tools, CPU pressure, or socket spikes on any device. The team's belief that "a leaf-to-spine cable failed overnight" is unconfirmed: no interface is down, no carrier-change history is flagged, no flap process exists, and connectivity is healthy. Root cause: no anomaly exists in the live topology; the reported symptom is either resolved or was never present.
Fix: No corrective action is required — the fabric is fully operational. The user should re-verify connectivity; if intermittent loss is suspected, deploy continuous monitoring to capture the transient event.
